  "query_preview": "/Friday through Tuesday/... \nBy Friday, the main upper level trough is forecast to shift \nnortheastward out of a our region leaving dry southwesterly flow in \nplace. Guidance shows low level mixing ratios quickly dropping to 5-\n7 g/kg across the lower deserts to only 6-8 g/kg over the Arizona \nhigh terrain. As a result, Friday will begin another dry period with \nonly lingering chances for some isolated showers and maybe a few \nthunderstorms over the eastern Arizona high terrain. Temperatures \nare forecast to remain quite stable over the next few days with \nhighs right around to slightly above normal and lows a few degrees \nabove normal.\n\nWesterly dry flow should persist through at least Saturday with \nlarge scale troughing remaining to our northwest while the \nsubtropical ridge slowly shifts southwestward from east Texas into \nthe interior of Mexico. This shift in the ridge is likely to bring \nhigher heights temporarily for our region later Friday through \nSaturday, so daytime highs Saturday are likely to top out between \n100-104 degrees (4-6 degrees above normal) under mostly sunny skies.\n\nForecast uncertainty increases dramatically going into early next \nweek as it will likely depend upon what happens with Hurricane Polo, \nthe positioning of the subtropical ridge, and an upper level trough \nthat may swing through our region on or around next Monday. The \nlatest NHC track for Polo has it slowly making its way westward over \nthe next few days before turning more to the north this weekend, but \nstaying west of southern Baja. This track may be enough to help \nadvect tropical moisture northward as far as our area, but \nuncertainty remains quite high. There is a low end potential of \ntropical moisture making its way northward into our area just before \nthe aforementioned trough swings through our region and this could \nlead to chances for strong to severe storms along with heavy rain \npotential on or around Monday. The more likely scenario would \ninvolve modest moisture making its way into our area, but still with \nthe support of the trough. The trough may still be strong enough to \nsupport some strong to severe storms due to much higher than normal \nshear, but with less moisture it would lower the severe threat as \nwell as well as the threat for heavy rainfall. At this moment, we \nare unsure which solution will win out, but we expect to have a \nbetter idea by the weekend.",
